package cajeta.lang;

/**
 * Text encoding identifiers for `String` byte-interchange.
 *
 * Every conversion across the bytes ↔ text boundary names its `Encoding`
 * explicitly — there is no platform-default fallback (Java's `FileReader`
 * trap). Pass one of these constants whenever you serialize or decode text.
 *
 * ## Usage
 * The byte ↔ text methods that consume these constants (`String.getBytes`,
 * `String.fromBytes`, and the transcoding hooks on `FileReader`) are not
 * yet implemented — see `String` for the phase plan. Until they land, an
 * `Encoding` value is simply selected by name:
 *
 * ```cajeta
 * Encoding wire = Encoding.UTF_8;
 * ```
 *
 * Decode-error behaviour, once those methods exist, is governed separately
 * by `EncodingErrorPolicy`.
 *
 * ## Coverage
 * v1 covers UTF + ASCII + Western (`LATIN_1`, `WINDOWS_1252`) + the four
 * most common CJK encodings. Arabic / Hebrew / additional ISO code pages
 * land in v2 if anybody asks. The cryptographic / compression family is
 * not part of `Encoding` — that lives under `cajeta.codec.*` (for example
 * `Json`), a separate package with no concrete crypto type yet.
 *
 * See docs/stdlib/lang/String.md § Encoding interchange.
 */
public enum Encoding {
    /** Variable-width Unicode, the default interchange encoding. */
    UTF_8,
    /** UTF-16, little-endian byte order. */
    UTF_16_LE,
    /** UTF-16, big-endian byte order. */
    UTF_16_BE,
    /** UTF-32, little-endian byte order. */
    UTF_32_LE,
    /** UTF-32, big-endian byte order. */
    UTF_32_BE,
    /** 7-bit US-ASCII; bytes above `0x7F` are invalid. */
    ASCII,
    /** ISO-8859-1 Latin-1 Western European. */
    LATIN_1,
    /** Windows codepage 1252, Western European. */
    WINDOWS_1252,
    /** GB18030 Simplified Chinese, covering the full Unicode range. */
    GB18030,
    /** Shift JIS Japanese. */
    SHIFT_JIS,
    /** EUC-KR Korean. */
    EUC_KR,
    /** Big5 Traditional Chinese. */
    BIG_5
}
